        'Glossary' => [
            'body' => '<h4>IFRS 9 measurement</h4><ul><li><b>ECL, expected credit loss</b>. The provision: exposure multiplied by the probability of default and the loss given default, discounted at the effective interest rate for the booked figure.</li><li><b>Booked ECL</b>. The ECL that is booked as the allowance: the loss discounted at each loan\'s effective interest rate. The Dashboard shows <b>Total ECL (booked)</b> only when every ECL run of the month was discounted.</li><li><b>Discounted ECL</b>. The ECL with each future loss brought to present value at the effective interest rate. The same as the booked ECL.</li><li><b>Undiscounted ECL</b>. The loss before discounting. Shown as <b>Total ECL (undiscounted)</b> when a run of the month was saved without discounting; it is not the booked figure.</li><li><b>EAD, exposure at default</b>. The amount owed if the borrower defaults: the carrying amount plus any undrawn commitment multiplied by the utilisation rate.</li><li><b>PD, probability of default</b>. The chance of default, over twelve months for Stage 1 and over the remaining life for Stages 2 and 3.</li><li><b>LGD, loss given default</b>. The share of exposure not recovered after collateral and recoveries.</li><li><b>Stage 1</b>. Performing. Twelve-month expected credit loss.</li><li><b>Stage 2</b>. Under-performing after a significant increase in credit risk. Lifetime expected credit loss.</li><li><b>Stage 3</b>. Credit-impaired, in default. Lifetime expected credit loss.</li><li><b>SICR, significant increase in credit risk</b>. The trigger that moves an exposure from Stage 1 to Stage 2.</li><li><b>DPD, days past due</b>. How long a payment has been outstanding. It drives the quantitative stage and the prudential class.</li><li><b>Amortised cost</b>. The carrying amount built up as opening balance plus effective interest less cash received.</li><li><b>Coverage ratio</b>. Expected credit loss divided by exposure, shown as a percentage.</li><li><b>Observed default rate</b>. The share of the Stage 1 and 2 balance at the start of a window that defaulted or left the book unpaid during it.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Models</h4><ul><li><b>Transition matrix</b>. The probabilities of moving between grades or stages over a period; the basis of the probability of default.</li><li><b>Transition profile</b>. The configuration that decides which data and grading columns build a matrix, and whether it counts loans or balances.</li><li><b>Cumulative matrix</b>. Monthly matrices chained together to give multi-period, and therefore lifetime, probabilities.</li><li><b>Internal grade</b>. The master risk scale mapped from the twelve-month probability.</li><li><b>Cure rate</b>. The share of Stage 3 exposure restored to performing.</li><li><b>Recovery rate</b>. The share of defaulted exposure recovered.</li><li><b>CCF, credit conversion factor</b>. The share of an undrawn commitment expected to be drawn at default. The platform uses the facility utilisation rate.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Forward-looking</h4><ul><li><b>FLI, forward-looking information</b>. The macroeconomic adjustment that turns a historical probability into a forward-looking one.</li><li><b>Macro element</b>. An economic variable such as inflation or the exchange rate, held with its values by month.</li><li><b>Scenario set</b>. The economic scenarios used for one reporting month (for example Upside, Base, Downside, Severe) with their probability weights, which must sum to one hundred per cent. One person proposes the set and a second approves it; only an approved set drives the ECL.</li><li><b>Scenario weight</b>. The probability attached to one scenario of the set.</li><li><b>Regression</b>. A fitted relationship between historical credit loss and macro variables, used to convert a macro movement into a probability adjustment. A fit is proposed and approved; approval publishes it as the model version in force.</li><li><b>Overlay</b>. A management adjustment applied on top of the modelled result, recorded with its reason, proposed by one person and approved by another, and shown separately in the ECL build-up.</li><li><b>FLI month record</b>. The record of a month\'s forward-looking close: the ECL under each scenario, the probability-weighted ECL, its reconciliation to the booked ECL and what it ran under. It is <b>recorded</b> first, then <b>signed off</b> by a second person; the Workspace step Forward-looking month-end is done only after sign-off.</li><li><b>Probability-weighted ECL</b>. The ECL under each scenario multiplied by its weight and added up.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Interest and revenue</h4><ul><li><b>EIR, effective interest rate</b>. The rate that exactly discounts the contractual cash flows, including integral fees, to the initial net investment.</li><li><b>CIR, contractual interest rate</b>. The rate stated in the offer letter, before fees.</li><li><b>Integral fee</b>. A fee or cost directly attributable to originating the loan, and therefore part of the effective interest rate. Other fees are period income.</li><li><b>Held GL line</b>. A fee posting in the general ledger that could not be placed on one loan. It is held on Fee Classification until a person ties it to its loan with a reason; nothing is placed by guess.</li><li><b>Tie</b>. Two meanings: on Fee Classification, to <b>tie</b> a held GL fee line to its loan (<b>Tie to the loan</b>, with a reason); on reports and the Dashboard, a figure <b>ties</b> when two sources of it agree (a green <b>Ties</b> badge), and <b>differs</b> when they do not.</li><li><b>Take-on</b>. Bringing loans that pre-date the system in from Finance\'s take-on workbooks: the original schedules and the mapping to the E-Banker accounts. Seen on Imports &amp; Feeds, Take-on Blocks and Take-on Population.</li><li><b>Write-off register</b>. The register on EIR Data, Write-offs for a write-off that is not on the loan account (for example one made only at GL level). One person proposes it with evidence, a different person approves it; it is then treated as derecognition, not cash. Write-offs that E-Banker posts to the loan account need no approval.</li><li><b>Restructure</b>. A change to a loan\'s terms. The system lists <b>candidates</b> found in E-Banker\'s reschedule history; a candidate is only evidence until one person classifies it and a different person confirms it on EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition, Restructures &amp; Resets.</li><li><b>Rate reset</b>. A move in a floating loan\'s rate, either with the prime lending rate (PLR) or by a change of spread. Listed in the rate-reset register on Restructures &amp; Resets; a reset is not a restructure.</li><li><b>Unwind</b>. The interest recognised on the loss allowance of a Stage 3 exposure as time passes.</li><li><b>Readiness</b>. The named conditions a contract must satisfy before its effective interest rate can be solved; what is missing shows as a blocker.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Data and structure</h4><ul><li><b>Reporting period</b>. The month-end loan book snapshot, written as year and month, that every screen is scoped to.</li><li><b>Period lock</b>. A reporting period locked on Imports &amp; Feeds, Periods Built, once its month-end is signed off. Nothing restates a locked month: the loan book build, staging, the PD, the LGD, the forward-looking route, the ECL, the revenue roll-forward and write-offs all refuse to write it. Locking and unlocking each need a reason and are audit logged.</li><li><b>Financial period</b>. The accounting period the organisation posts into, open or closed. Not the same as a reporting period.</li><li><b>Portfolio</b>. A segment of the book, used by every stage table and roll-forward.</li><li><b>Mega Farm</b>. The Mega Farm programme: government money MAIIC manages on the government\'s behalf. Its loans are kept in their own programme portfolios, measured by their own engine on their own PD and LGD, and shown separately (ECL Processing, Mega Farm Programme, and a line of the ECL build-up).</li><li><b>Sector</b>. The economic sector of the borrower, used for concentration reporting.</li><li><b>Product group</b>. The lending product family.</li><li><b>Collateral allocation</b>. Attaching a collateral item, at its discounted value, to a specific exposure.</li><li><b>Forced-sale discount</b>. The haircut applied to a collateral valuation to reflect what a quick sale would realise.</li><li><b>E-Banker pack</b>. The zipped set of core-banking extracts with a manifest, landed through gates on Imports &amp; Feeds; a loan book build is then proposed from it and approved by a second person.</li><li><b>Control total</b>. An account balance from the trial balance that all detail must reconcile to.</li><li><b>Golden number</b>. A fixed expected result pinned by a test so that any change to a calculation is noticed.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Prudential</h4><ul><li><b>RBM classification</b>. The Reserve Bank of Malawi prudential classes: Pass, Special Mention, Substandard, Doubtful and Loss.</li><li><b>NPL, non-performing loan</b>. Substandard, Doubtful and Loss taken together, ninety or more days past due.</li><li><b>HHI, Herfindahl-Hirschman index</b>. A concentration measure; above 2,500 counts as highly concentrated.</li><li><b>Regulatory reserve</b>. The excess of the prudential provision over the IFRS 9 provision, where the directive requires more.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Governance</h4><ul><li><b>Maker-checker</b>. Two people for every governed change: one proposes (the maker), a different one approves (the checker). The system refuses an approval by the proposer. Used for governance settings, staging thresholds, fee classification, EIR approval and lock, write-offs, restructures, loan book builds, scenario sets, regression fits, model versions, overlays, FLI month records and compliance sections.</li><li><b>Governance setting</b>. One calculation convention, kept as a record with a fixed list of options, an effective date, a proposer and an approver rather than as a line of code.</li><li><b>Value in force</b>. The approved option that applies on a date: the latest approved change whose effective date is on or before it.</li><li><b>Effective date</b>. The first day a change applies. It must be later than the last approved change, so a change never restates an earlier month.</li><li><b>Recorded, not yet applied</b>. A badge on a Governance Centre setting whose value is approved and kept but not yet read by the engines; the setting explains what the engine does instead.</li><li><b>Superseded value</b>. A value replaced by a later approved change. It stays in the history and still governs the months before the change.</li><li><b>Draft</b> and <b>Lock</b>. A draft can be changed; a locked record is final and needs a deliberate, recorded reopening.</li></ul>',
        ],

        'Troubleshooting and FAQ' => [
            'body' => '<h4>Signing in</h4><ul><li><b>The security code is rejected.</b> Codes are single use and expire after five minutes. Press the circular-arrows button beside the image and type the new code. The code is not case sensitive.</li><li><b>The security code image does not appear.</b> Report it to ICT: the server component that draws it is unavailable.</li><li><b>My password is correct but sign-in fails.</b> Either the credentials do not match, and the page says so, or your account has been removed. Ask an administrator.</li><li><b>Too many attempts.</b> After five failures in a minute further attempts are refused for a short while. Wait a minute and try again.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Finding things</h4><ul><li><b>A page shows no data.</b> Check the reporting period first. A month with no calculation shows a message, not zeros.</li><li><b>I cannot see a sidebar entry.</b> Your role does not carry its permission; the sidebar hides it. Ask an administrator.</li><li><b>An entry I can see answers No access rights.</b> The screen needs a further permission (for example Audit Trail and Settings need <code>settings</code>). Ask an administrator.</li><li><b>The sidebar is folded to icons.</b> Press the right chevron (<b>Expand the menu</b>) at its foot.</li><li><b>A sidebar block keeps opening.</b> It holds the page you are on.</li><li><b>The top Search box finds nothing.</b> It does not search in this release; use the search box of each list.</li><li><b>The session expired.</b> Sign in again; unsaved form input is lost, so save long entries as you go.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Approvals and locks</h4><ul><li><b>Approve is greyed out or missing.</b> You proposed the item. A different person must approve it; that is the control working.</li><li><b>Where is everything waiting for me?</b> Workspace, <b>Awaiting approval</b> tab: each line links to the screen where it is approved.</li><li><b>Reporting period 2025-11 is locked</b>. The month is locked so nothing restates it. If the restatement is deliberate, an authorised user unlocks it with a reason on Imports &amp; Feeds, Periods Built.</li><li><b>The Forward-looking month-end step stays Pending.</b> The month must be recorded after the ECL and signed off by a second person on FLI Month-End, Overview.</li><li><b>A Governance Centre setting shows recorded, not yet applied.</b> The engines do not read that value yet; the setting\'s note says what they do instead.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Figures</h4><ul><li><b>The Dashboard says Total ECL (undiscounted).</b> A run of the month, or of the comparison month, was saved without discounting at the EIR. Run the ECL again with discounting.</li><li><b>A gold Differs badge.</b> Two sources of the same figure disagree, for example the loans\' booked ECL and the saved runs. Run the ECL for the month again; if it persists, raise a support ticket.</li><li><b>Two reports disagree.</b> Confirm both are on the same reporting period and the same scope. A portfolio-level run and a whole-book run cover different populations.</li><li><b>Non-performing loans do not equal Stage 3.</b> They are different definitions: Stage 3 is the IFRS 9 credit-impaired population; NPL is the prudential ninety-day classification.</li><li><b>Coverage jumped this month.</b> Look at the stage split and Where the ECL sits first: one large exposure moving to Stage 3 moves coverage more than any model change.</li><li><b>No stress run for a month on the Dashboard.</b> Record the forward-looking month, or run the scenario set on Stress Testing.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Calculations</h4><ul><li><b>An ECL calculation is already running.</b> Another run holds the lock for that month and scope. Wait for it to finish; a job started from Maintenance shows under <b>Now running</b> there.</li><li><b>I cannot edit a transition matrix or a loss given default run.</b> It is locked for audit integrity. Create a new draft instead.</li><li><b>An EIR contract is blocked.</b> Open EIR Calculations, Blockers and read the named blocker; each says what is missing, for example an unapproved schedule or an unreviewed fee line.</li><li><b>Records could not be discounted.</b> Those contracts have no approved and locked effective interest rate. Resolve them under EIR &amp; Revenue Recognition, then run again.</li><li><b>A held GL fee line will not go away.</b> It waits until someone ties it to its loan on Fee Classification.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Imports and files</h4><ul><li><b>My file was not imported and nothing was saved.</b> The check found errors. Read the problems table (row, column, problem, how to fix it), correct the file and choose it again. Use <b>Check file</b> first next time.</li><li><b>A column is listed under Columns not read.</b> Its heading differs from the sample file; rename it or use Match columns.</li><li><b>There is no Check file button.</b> Choose a file first; the take-on workbooks, the E-Banker pack and the S&amp;P workbook check themselves when they land.</li><li><b>My import stays at pending.</b> The background worker is not running. Ask ICT to start it; nothing is lost, the job resumes.</li><li><b>My import shows failed rows.</b> Download them from the History tab (grey download button); each carries its reason.</li><li><b>A PDF downloads blank.</b> Raise a support ticket naming the report and the month.</li></ul>',
        ],

        'Browser and printing notes' => [
            'body' => '<h4>Browsers</h4><p>The platform is built for current versions of Microsoft Edge, Google Chrome and Mozilla Firefox. It works on a laptop, and the layout narrows sensibly on a tablet; a phone is not a practical size for the wide tables. Keep your browser updated: an out-of-date browser is the usual cause of a page that renders oddly.</p><h4>Light mode only</h4><p>The platform is always shown in light mode. There is no appearance switch, and a device set to dark mode does not change it.</p><h4>Screen size and zoom</h4><p>Wide tables scroll sideways inside their own panel rather than stretching the page, so the sidebar and top bar stay put. If a table feels cramped, press <b>Collapse menu</b> at the foot of the sidebar to fold it to icons, or reduce the browser zoom with Ctrl and minus. On a narrow screen the sidebar becomes a drawer opened from the three-line button.</p><h4>What the browser remembers</h4><p>Which sidebar blocks and groups are folded, and whether the sidebar is folded to icons, are kept in this browser for you. On another computer or browser, or after clearing site data, the sidebar opens with its defaults again. Filters are kept in the page address, so a filtered view can be bookmarked.</p><h4>Printing</h4><p>Do not print a screen from the browser. Reports and the Dashboard carry a PDF button that produces the branded document with the logo, the generating user, the confidentiality note and page numbers. That is the version to circulate or file. The manuals have the same <b>Download PDF</b> button.</p><h4>Downloads</h4><p>CSV files open in Excel but hold plain rows. An <b>Excel</b> export produces a workbook where figures are real numbers, so use it rather than the CSV when someone will do arithmetic on the result.</p><h4>Sessions</h4><p>You are signed out after a period of inactivity. Save long entries as you go. Two-factor authentication, if you have enabled it, is asked for at sign-in, not again during the session.</p><h4>Confidentiality</h4><p>Every screen and export holds real customer names and balances. Downloaded files carry the same sensitivity as the system itself: store them where client data belongs and do not forward them outside the approved distribution.</p>',
        ],

    ],

];
